## At a glance

- **Problem:** A deletion workflow can report "Complete" while personal data survives in replicas, logs, exports, search indexes and backups. The task is to audit 518 deletion requests against messy evidence (SQL snapshots, JSON, six log formats, PDFs, screenshots) and output an evidence-cited verdict plus a remediation plan.
- **Approach:** Deterministic, testable Python core (identity resolution, evidence parsing, linking, state at `audit_at`, policy, planning). LLMs are confined to intake classification, screenshot transcription and ambiguous record links, behind validation gates.
- **Result (32 public sample cases):** {{sample.det.status_acc}} deletion-status accuracy, {{sample.agent.step_f1}} step F1, {{custom.passed}}/{{custom.total}} hand-written custom cases passing, {{tests.count}} pytest tests plus a byte-for-byte golden-output lock.
- **Zero-cost and reproducible:** free-tier models only; the committed cache reproduces the submitted `output.csv` with no API keys or network.

**Skills demonstrated:** agent design with tool registries and guardrails, evidence/time-bounded reasoning, LLM output validation, test-first development, evaluation harness design, prompt-injection handling, reproducible pipelines.

> **Dataset note:** the challenge dataset and problem statement belong to HackerRank and are not redistributed here. To run the pipeline, place the official `dataset/` folder at the repository root (see Quickstart).

